## Background and Motivation

To provide a way to register object pools of scoped dependencies that will be created using DI resolution.
Pooled objects can then be reused across requests.

A custom option object provides a way to configure the pool size externally.

## Proposed API

```c#
namespace Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Pools;

public static class DependencyInjectionExtensions
{
    public static IServiceCollection AddPool<TDefinition>(this IServiceCollection services, Action<PoolOptions>? configure = null) 
        where TDefinition : class

    public static IServiceCollection AddPool<TDefinition, TImplementation>(this IServiceCollection services, Action<PoolOptions>? configure = null)
        where TDefinition : class
        where TImplementation : class, TDefinition

    public static IServiceCollection ConfigurePools(this IServiceCollection services, IConfigurationSection section)
}

public sealed class PoolOptions
{
    public int Capacity { get; set; } = Environment.ProcessorCount * 2;
}
```

Internally an `ObjectPool<T>` is registered as a singleton and uses a custom `IPooledObjectPolicy` that uses the service provider to create new instances (referred as `DependencyInjectedPooledObjectPolicy` later in this document).

## Usage Examples

### Resolving the pool and using it directly

```c#
services
    .AddPool<MyService>()
    .AddScoped<Consumer>()
    ;
```

```c#
public class Consumer
{
    private readonly MyService _myService;
    
    public Consumer(ObjectPool<MyService> myServicePool)
    {
        var myService = myServicePool.Get();
        
        myService.DoSomething();

        myServicePool.Return(myService);
    }
}

public class MyService : IDisposable, IResettable
{
    // Lots of properties
    // ...

    private readonly ILogger _logger;

    public MyService(ILogger<MyService> logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;

        // Expensive initialization
        // ...
    }

    public void DoSomething()
    {
        // ...
        logger.Debug("Something was done");
    }

    public bool TryReset()
    {
        // ...
        return true;
    }
}
```

### Resolving a pooled instance without prior knowledge of pools:

```c#
services
    .AddPool<PooledFeature>()
    .AddScoped(provider => provider.GetRequiredService<ObjectPool<PooledFeature>>().Get())
    .AddScoped(provider => provider.GetRequiredService<PooledFeature>().Feature)
    ;
```

```c#
internal sealed class PooledFeature : IDisposable
{
    private readonly ObjectPool<PooledFeature> _pool;
    public Feature Feature { get; }
    
    public PoolHelper(ObjectPool<PooledFeature> pool, ILogger<CustomFeature> logger)
    {
        _pool = pool;
        Feature = new Feature(logger);
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Feature.Reset();
        _pool.Return(this);
    }
}
```

## Alternative Designs

### Expose the policy publicly only

[This article](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/performance/objectpool?view=aspnetcore-7.0#objectpool-sample) describes how to use `ObjectPool<T>` with a `StringBuilder` like this: 

```c#
builder.Services.TryAddSingleton<ObjectPoolProvider, DefaultObjectPoolProvider>();
builder.Services.TryAddSingleton<ObjectPool<StringBuilder>>(serviceProvider =>
{
    var provider = serviceProvider.GetRequiredService<ObjectPoolProvider>();
    var policy = new Microsoft.Extensions.ObjectPool.StringBuilderPooledObjectPolicy();
    return provider.Create(policy);
});
```

An alternative design to the proposal would be to only provide a custom `DependencyInjectedPooledObjectPolicy` as a public type to be used the same way for types that need to resolve dependencies from DI.

### Open generic

```c#
namespace Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Pools;

public static class DependencyInjectionExtensions
{
    public static IServiceCollection AddObjectPools(this IServiceCollection services);
}

public sealed class PoolOptions
{
    public int Capacity { get; set; } = Environment.ProcessorCount * 2;
}

namespaceMicrosoft.Extensions.ObjectPool;

public interface IObjectPool<T> where T : class
{
    T Get();
    void Return(T obj);
}
```

#### Usage:

```c#
var services = new ServiceCollection()
    .AddSingleton<TestDependency>()
    .AddScoped<ITestClass, TestClass>()
    .AddObjectPools();
    

var pool = provider.GetRequiredService<IObjectPool<ITestClass>>();
var pooled = pool .Get();
pool.Return(pooled);
```

#### Automatic pooled dependency

Optionally we can implement a usage that allows to resolve scoped dependencies directly that would be returned when the scope expires.

```c#
var services = new ServiceCollection()
  .AddSingleton<TestDependency>()
  .AddObjectPools()
  .AddScoped<TestClass>()
  .AddTransient<Pooled<TestClass>>()
  .AddScoped<ITestClass>(provider =>
  {
    var pooled = provider.GetRequiredService<Pooled<TestClass>>();
    return pooled.Object;
  });
;

using (var scope = provider.CreateScope())
{
    resolved1 = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<ITestClass>();
    resolved2 = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<ITestClass>();
}

Assert.True(resolved1 == resolved2);
Assert.Equal(1, resolved1.DisposedCalled);
Assert.Equal(1, resolved1.ResetCalled);
```

## Risks

- Where should it go since it depends on M.E.ObjectPool and M.E.DependencyInjection ?
- Pooling objects with scoped/transient dependencies might leak services across requests that are not meant to be reused.
- As with any pooled objects, not returning instances correctly would invalidate the use of pools.

